# resize-transient — looprs-zie
# measured 2026-10-08, aarch64-apple-darwin (Apple Silicon), macOS, debug profile
#
# commands:
#   # the shipped configuration: store cap 32 MiB, per-view transcript buffer 256 KiB
#   LOOPRS_MEASURE_CORPUS=~/.pi/agent/sessions/--Users-danielstahl-Documents-code-ml-personal-harness-pi-beads-looprs-- \
#   cargo test --bin looprs -- --ignored --nocapture --test-threads=1 resize_transient
#
#   # the ticket's configuration: transcript buffer off, so the whole pass stays in
#   # memory the way an uncapped view holds it
#   LOOPRS_MEASURE_BUFFER=0 LOOPRS_MEASURE_CORPUS=~/.pi/agent/sessions/--Users-danielstahl-Documents-code-ml-personal-harness-pi-beads-looprs-- \
#   cargo test --bin looprs -- --ignored --nocapture --test-threads=1 resize_transient
#
# "peak" is the high-water mark of live heap *inside* the rebuild, counted by the
# same CountingAllocator the pdl.7 run used, with the window opened after the
# build-up: the row that matters is what one width change adds on top of what was
# already resident, because that is what a window drag pays. The new rows are all
# made before the old ones are freed, so the peak is the sum of both, which is
# the transient under measurement rather than a footprint after it.
#
# The two sections are the same corpus at the same widths:
#   * "rewrap at the cap the code sets" — the source cut (this ticket's fix):
#     only the newest slice of the transcript that the cap can afford is
#     re-rendered.
#   * "the same corpus with no store cap" — what rendering the whole source
#     costs, which is the old shape's transient too: the trim runs after the
#     render, so a cap on the store does not cap the peak of a full re-render.
#
# FINDING: a rewrap of this corpus cost 133–151 MiB of transient to keep 32 MiB.
# With the source cut it costs 9–25 MiB, and it does not grow with the length of
# the transcript. In the shipped configuration (256 KiB view buffer) the cap is
# not reached at all by this corpus, so both shapes read the same: the ticket is
# about the case that *does* reach it — a long transcript, or short dense lines,
# where the old code paid the transcript's cost and threw most of it away.

